{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>And Laban said, It is not done so, etc.</b> One needs to know what answer there is in his saying ״לֹא יֵעָשֶׂה״ and so forth — for was not the matter a stipulation: ״I will serve you for Rachel״? And what relevance has the local custom here, in a matter that was a full stipulation?" "<b>Rather,</b> he intended to say that the men of the place objected to his doing such a thing, and this is his saying <b>It is not done so in our place</b> — for it is the claim of the many." "<b>Further,</b> it appears that his claim is that it was not explicitly stated at the time of the stipulation that even if Leah were not to be married he would give him Rachel. And in this there is no contradiction between the custom and the stipulation, for in any case Leah takes precedence to be married, and upon the completion of the seven years that Jacob would serve, if Leah preceded and was married — immediately he would take Rachel; and if not, he would sit and wait until the elder one preceded according to the laws of the land, for there is nothing in the stipulation indicating that they agreed to annul the custom. From now on, when you said ״Give me my wife,״ I thought you meant Leah, for the custom is a well-known custom that the younger may not be given first, and from the unspecified reference you meant Leah, and there is no deceit in this. And it was incumbent upon you to know that she was Leah without need of being told — and thereby the claim of ״you deceived me״ is annulled. And if you consider that there is a difference between them, it is not so, for they are equal in my eyes, and I do not ask on behalf of Rachel except seven years. And if because of your desire for Rachel you are insistent about the delay — behold, she is given to you immediately after the seven days of Leah's feast, and this is his saying <b>Complete the week of this one</b> — meaning, the seven days of the feast — and immediately I will give you Rachel, in advance, for the service that you will serve another seven years. And this was Jacob's desired outcome, for he accepted upon himself not to send Leah back, heaven forbid." "And we need to know: how did Jacob not take heed beforehand of this claim, that it is not the way to give the younger and so forth? One may say that he thought Laban was obligated to disclose the matter to him, and without this, this matter is not of the kind of insistence that is binding when the father wishes to give the younger. And when he asked ״Give me my wife,״ from the unspecified reference he meant Rachel, and Laban should have said to him that he could not give him Rachel before Leah was married — and the matter would have been in Jacob's hands: either to wait until Leah was married, or to take Leah. And this is nothing but from HaShem — ״Laban the Aramean״ — and all the claims that I have written have a contradiction to them, and his name will tell about him."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:45:01.822398699Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}